# The read direction, made to run (panel 059, ratified 2026-08-15).
#
# `tests/golden/run/` had **zero** coverage of a `cstr` coming back — the only null
# case was `abort-null-cstr-into-c.hero`, which is the *outbound* direction and never
# calls `to_str`. That gap is why the sitting had to measure the abort by hand.
#
# Three facts the spec's FFI clause states, one program each:
#   1. `to_str` COPIES — the `str` survives the next call into C, which is what makes
#      `getenv`, `ctime`, `strerror` and `sqlite3_column_text` bindable at all;
#   2. `c == nullptr` is testable BEFORE converting, which is the escape the clause
#      tells the reader to take;
#   3. a guarded read of a genuinely absent value takes the null branch.
#
# `strerror` is the witness because its buffer is documented by POSIX as
# *"invalidated or overwritten by a subsequent call"* — so if `to_str` borrowed, the
# two `str` values would become EQUAL after the second call, both looking at one
# buffer holding the second message.
#
# **Nothing platform-specific is printed, deliberately** — and the first version of
# this file got that half right and half wrong, which is why the codes are `1` and
# `2` rather than two large ones. It printed no libc text, and then rested on a
# libc *property*: that two **unknown** codes give two different messages. They do
# on Darwin and glibc, which embed the number (`Unknown error: 9999`), and they do
# not on the MSVC CRT, which answers one generic string for every out-of-range
# code. So `first == second` was `false` on two legs and `true` on the third, and
# the case went red on Windows for a reason that was not the compiler's — the exact
# failure its own paragraph claimed to have avoided (fixed 2026-08-15).
#
# `1` and `2` are `EPERM` and `ENOENT`, the two errno values C11 §7.5 requires
# every implementation to define, and no libc gives them the same message. The
# relationship being asserted — two calls, two independent strings — is unchanged;
# what is gone is the premise about how a libc renders a code it does not know.
#
# `HOME` is still avoided for the reason it always was: it is not set on the
# Windows leg, so the absent-variable witness is a name no machine has.

extern "string.h"
    function strerror(code: i32) -> cstr

extern "stdlib.h"
    function getenv(name: cstr) -> cstr

function main()
    first = strerror(code: 1).validated().must()
    second = strerror(code: 2).validated().must()
    # `false` here is the whole point: a borrow would make these one string.
    print(first == second)
    print(len(first) > 0)

    # **The null test is GONE and that is the repair** (panel 090). This golden
    # was written for `to_str`, which aborts on a null pointer, so it had to
    # guard — and panel 087 measured that the guard does not save you anyway,
    # because ill-formed bytes abort too. `validated` answers both as a `str?`,
    # so the branch is where it belongs: on the value, not before it.
    absent = getenv(name: "HEROES_NO_SUCH_VARIABLE_059".cstr())
    match absent.validated()
        .ok text => print(text)
        .err e => print("absent: ", e.code)
